## ABSTRACT
It has been of interest in seeking electrocatalysts that could exercise equally high-efficient and durable hydrogen evolution upon nonselective electrolytes in both acidic and alkaline environments. Herein, we report a facile strategy to fabricate cobalt tungsten phosphides \((\mathsf{C o}_{x}\mathsf{W}_{2-x}\mathsf{P}_{2}/\mathsf{C})\) hollow polyhedrons with tunable composition based on metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) template method. By the deliberate control of W doping, the synthesized catalyst with the composition of \(\mathsf{C o}_{0.9}\mathsf{W}_{1.1}\mathsf{P}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\) is found to be able to achieve a current density of \(10\;\mathsf{m A}\cdot\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) at overpotentials of 35 and \(66\;\mathsf{m V}\) in acidic and alkaline media, respectively. This combined electrochemical property stands atop the state-of-the-art electrocatalyst counterparts. To unveil the peculiar behavior of the structure, density functional theory (DFT) calculation was implemented and reveals that the surface W-doping facilitates the optimization of hydrogen absorption free energy \((\Delta G_{\mathsf{H}^{+}})\) as well as the thermodynamic and kinetics barriers for water dissociation, which is coupled with the hollow structure of Co-W phosphides, leading to the prominent HER catalytic performance.
## KEYWORDS
cobalt tungsten phosphide, W-doping, tunable composition, hydrogen evolution reaction, acid and alkaline media
## 1 Introduction
Developing high-efficiency and robust electrocatalysts to facilitate hydrogen production is a significant solution to cope with both energy and climate crisis [1]. Hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is a key step for electrochemical water splitting to produce high-purity hydrogen [2]. Pt-based catalysts have been proved to be capable of significantly improving the electron-to-hydrogen conversion efficiency for HER [3]. However, high cost and scarcity of Pt are the main issues to limit its wide applications. As a consequence, the development of high-efficiency Pt-substitutes from earth-abundant elements has drawn great attention. Recently, various transition metal-based alloys, oxides, sulfides, phosphides and selenides are all potential active electrocatalysts for HER [4–11]. Among them, transition metal phosphides (TMPs) are regarded as the most promising ones due to their hydrogenase-like catalytic mechanism [12–14].To further increase the intrinsic activity of certain TMPs, doping another element into TMPs has been widely reported to improve their HER catalytic activities by tuning their surface electronic structures to optimize the hydrogen absorption properties [15–18]. It is demonstrated that the incorporation of Mo element into cobalt or nickel phosphides is an effective approach to boost their HER activity via electronic structure modulation [19–21]. Similar to Mo, W could also lead to the downshift in energy level of \(\mathrm{e_{g}}\) orbitals attributing to its strong electron-donating capacity, which is beneficial to enhance the hydrogen absorption [22–24]. However, traditional methods by introducing W into 3d transition metal phosphides (CoP, \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\), NiP, FeP, etc.) W is challenging due to the phase-separation tendency [25]. To address this concern, several ternary TMPs containing 3d transition metal and W (Mo) element have been successfully fabricated with remarkable HER catalytic activity [26–28]. Furthermore, the composition tuning in ternary TMPs is more accessible than binary TMPs ascribed to the higher complexity, which makes them promising candidates as HER electrocatalysts.In addition, extensive efforts have been made to promote the catalytic performance by structural engineering in order to creating more active sites and efficient channels for electron transfer in a robust frame with a high surface to volume ratio, which could facilitate a better catalytic kinetics and stability [29–31]. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are known as perfect sacrificial templates and metal precursors to prepare well-defined hollow structures. Variety of MOF-derived species have been developed with precisely control at molecular scale and successfully applied in many fields owing to their fast mass transport, excellent conductivity, high surface area and so on [32–37]. Moreover, MOFs precursors can serve as a carbon source to further promote the electronic conductivity and improve the free energy of hydrogen absorption.In this study, a combined strategy including MOFs-template
To further increase the intrinsic activity of certain TMPs, doping another element into TMPs has been widely reported to improve their HER catalytic activities by tuning their surface electronic structures to optimize the hydrogen absorption properties [15–18]. It is demonstrated that the incorporation of Mo element into cobalt or nickel phosphides is an effective approach to boost their HER activity via electronic structure modulation [19–21]. Similar to Mo, W could also lead to the downshift in energy level of \(\mathrm{e_{g}}\) orbitals attributing to its strong
electron-donating capacity, which is beneficial to enhance the hydrogen absorption [22–24]. However, traditional methods by introducing W into 3d transition metal phosphides (CoP, \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P},\) NiP, FeP, etc.) W is challenging due to the phase-separation tendency [25]. To address this concern, several ternary TMPs containing 3d transition metal and W (Mo) element have been successfully fabricated with remarkable HER catalytic activity [26–28]. Furthermore, the composition tuning in ternary TMPs is more accessible than binary TMPs ascribed to the higher complexity, which makes them promising candidates as HER electrocatalysts.
In addition, extensive efforts have been made to promote the catalytic performance by structural engineering in order to creating more active sites and efficient channels for electron transfer in a robust frame with a high surface to volume ratio, which could facilitate a better catalytic kinetics and stability [29–31]. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are known as perfect sacrificial templates and metal precursors to prepare well-defined hollow structures. Variety of MOF-derived species have been developed with precisely control at molecular scale and successfully applied in many fields owing to their fast mass transport, excellent conductivity, high surface area and so on [32–37]. Moreover, MOFs precursors can serve as a carbon source to further promote the electronic conductivity and improve the free energy of hydrogen absorption.
In this study, a combined strategy including MOFs-template
method and composition tuning is employed to fabricate carbon-incorporated \(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{W}_{2-x}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) hollow polyhedrons with tunable composition. The density functional theory (DFT) calculation shows that the tuned \(\mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) presents a \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}^{+}}\) of \(0.0\ \mathrm{eV}\), which is even superior to Pt. Experimental electrochemical evaluation indicates that the \(\mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) polyhedrons HER catalyst could reach a current density of \(10\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}}\) at \(-35\) and \(-54\ \mathrm{mV}\) (vs. RHE) in both acid and alkaline media, respectively.
## 2 Results and discussion
The morphology and structure of the as-fabricated Co-W precursors were characterized by electron microscopic technique. It is seen that the surface of Co-W precursor polyhedrons are constructed with numerous interconnected nanosheets (Fig. S2 in the ESM), and the partial destroyed shell demonstrates their hollow structure. Scanning transmission electron microscopy-energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (STEM-EDX) mapping suggests that elements C, O, Co and W are homogeneously distributed across the hollow Co-W precursor particle and the nanosheets (Fig. S3 in the ESM). After the pyrolysis process, the overall morphology of Co-W precursor polyhedrons are spotted to be well preserved except the slight shrinkage of the nanosheets during the phosphorization process (Fig. 2(a)). The uniform distribution of \(\mathrm{P}\) element instead of O element over the \(\mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) polyhedron particle (Fig. 2(b)) proved by STEM-EDX mapping indicates the successful conversion of Co-W precursor to cobalt tungsten phosphide. The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image that was taken from a single-crystalline nanosheet clearly reveals the coverage of a thin amorphous carbon layer over the surface of \(\mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) polyhedron (Fig. 2(c)). In addition, the fast fourier transform (FFT) pattern derived from the crystalline nanosheet matches that of \(\mathrm{CoWP}_{2}\) (ICSD 01-081-4849) in the projection plane of (001). X-ray diffraction patterns in Fig. 2(d) demonstrate that three Co-W phosphides present identical \(\mathrm{CoWP}_{2}\) crystalline structure. Due to the increase of W to Co atomic ratio and the larger atomic radius of W than Co, the substitution of Co atoms by W atoms within \(\mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) brings about the lattice expansion leading to the slight shift of the diffracted peaks towards the low angle direction. This is further confirmed by the \(d\)-spacing of (100) lattice plane of \(\mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) polyhedron, which is measured to be \(0.288\ \mathrm{nm}\) which is slightly larger than \(0.283\ \mathrm{nm}\) of the standard \(\mathrm{CoWP}_{2}\) quoted from the database record. The specific surface area and porosity of \(\mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) were measured by the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) and Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) method, respectively. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption isotherms (Fig. S5(a) in the ESM) and the pore size distribution curves (Fig. S5(b) in the ESM) suggest a \(32.5\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) BET surface area and a mesoporous structure, respectively. The higher surface area is expected to provide more catalytic active sites while the mesoporous structure could facilitate the rapid charge and mass transport.
