The compositions of carbon incorporated Co-W phosphides were further performed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), as shown in Fig. 3. The ICP-AES results indicate that the finely controlled solvothermal reaction followed by the phosphorization process successfully produced three cobalt tungsten phosphides with the \(\mathrm{Co/W}\) ratio of 1:1, 0.95:1.05 and 0.9:1.1, respectively (Fig. S4 in the ESM). The high-resolution C 1s spectrum (Fig. 3(a)) consists of a strong peak of C–C species \((284.5\ \mathrm{eV})\) and two relatively weak peaks of C–O–C \((286.0\ \mathrm{eV})\) and \(\mathrm{O-C=O}\) \((288.4\ \mathrm{eV})\), which confirms the incorporation of carbon into Co-W phosphides [38]. The C–O bonds are attributed to the adsorption of oxygen onto the Co-W-P/C polyhedrons surface. In the high resolution spectra of Co 2p (Fig. 3(b)), two spin-orbit doublets are characterized
as \( \mathrm{Co}^{2+} \) and \( \mathrm{Co}^{3+} \), coupled with two shake-up satellite peaks [39]. The W 4f spectra could also be deconvoluted into \( \mathrm{W}^{5+} \) and \( \mathrm{W}^{6+} \) two spin-orbit doublets [40], as shown in Fig. 3(c). Two distinct peaks at 129.4 and \( 130.2\ \mathrm{eV} \) (Fig. 3(d)) can be allocated to the \( \mathrm{P} \) \( 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2} \) and \( \mathrm{P} \) \( 2\mathrm{p}_{1/2} \), respectively, and another peak at \( 133.9\ \mathrm{eV} \) could be assigned to the surface oxidized phosphorus species as a result of air exposure. Raman spectrum of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) (Fig. S6 in the ESM) contains two broad peaks located at 1,398 and \( 1,582\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \), which are associated with typical D and G bands of carbon, respectively [41]. The small \( I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}} \) ratio indicates a high degree of graphitization in \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \), which renders \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) having a good electronic conduction.
The HER property of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) was first tested using a three-electrode system in \( 0.5\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4} \) solution. For comparison, three catalysts with similar structure, \( \mathrm{CoWP}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \), \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) and \( \mathrm{CoP}/\mathrm{C} \), decorated on CC were also measured. From the LSV curves in Figs. 4(a) and 4(b), the \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) exhibits only a small overpotential of \( 35\ \mathrm{mV} \) to reach the current density of \( 10\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2} \), which is only \( 10\ \mathrm{mV} \) higher than \( \mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C} \) \( (25\ \mathrm{mV}) \). Moreover, this value is lower than those of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) \( (59\ \mathrm{mV}) \) and \( \mathrm{CoWP}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) \( (76\ \mathrm{mV}) \), implying the controlled W doping is an effective approach to boost the HER activity of \( \mathrm{CoWP}_{2} \) in acid solution. According to Fig. 4(c), \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) exhibits the smallest Tafel slope \( (34\ \mathrm{mV\cdot dec}^{-1}) \) among all the Co-W phosphide catalysts, which is also comparable to that of \( \mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C} \), indicating its excellent HER reaction kinetics [42]. Furthermore, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was also employed to study their catalytic kinetics under HER operating conditions. As depicted in Fig. 4(d), the Nyquist plots of all catalysts present semicircular portion, where the radius of the semicircle in the high-frequency range is proportional to the charge-transfer resistance \( \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{ct}} \). Among the phosphide catalysts, the smallest radius of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) suggests its smallest \( \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{ct}} \) value (i.e. the fastest electrode reduction reaction rate) during the HER process, which is also in agreement with the linear sweep results above. In order to further investigate the enhanced electrochemical performance beneficial from composition tuning, electrochemical double layer capacitance \( \left(\mathrm{C_{dl}}\right) \) of three Co-W phosphide catalysts is measured to estimate their electrochemically active surface area (ECSA). As illustrated in Fig. S7 in the ESM, the calculated \( \mathrm{C_{dl}} \) of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) is \( 30.2\ \mathrm{mF\cdot cm}^{-2} \), which is much higher than those of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \)
\( (22.8\ \mathrm{mF\cdot cm}^{-2}) \) and \( \mathrm{CoWP}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) \( (14.7\ \mathrm{mF\cdot cm}^{-2}) \). The large \( \mathrm{C_{dl}} \) of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) is attributed to the abundant active sites on interconnected nanosheets controlled by tuning its atomic structure, achieving a very large ECSA. Moreover, the unique hollow structure that is full of macro-/mesopores is beneficial for fast mass transport as well as charge transfer.
More importantly, the \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) shows promising long-term stability (Fig. 4(f)) in acid solution after subject to a constant current density of \( 20\ \mathrm{mF\cdot cm}^{-2} \) for \( 50\ \mathrm{h} \). The LSV profile after long-term electrolysis also illustrates a negligible voltage decrease, indicating the superb stability of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) in acid solution (Fig. S8 in the ESM). The physical properties of the catalyst after the stability test was characterized by SEM observation (Fig. S9 in the ESM). It shows that the overall hollow polyhedral morphology can be preserved well after the durability test in acid electrolyte. In addition, ICP-AES was employed to retrace the stability of Co-W phosphides in acid electrolyte after long-term stability test \( (50\ \mathrm{h} \) at \( 20\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2}) \). The concentrations of dissolved Co, W and P are determined to be 0.1142, 0.1005 and \( 0.2176\ \upmu\mathrm{mmol/L} \), respectively (Fig. S10 in the ESM). In contrast, similar results are also obtained for the other two Co-W phosphides, where the concentration of dissolved Co is also slightly higher than that of W whilst the concentration of P is approximately the sum of Co and W concentrations, indicating the homogeneous dissolution of Co-W phosphides in acid electrolyte during the electrolysis process.
The HER property of the Co-W phosphides was also evaluated in \( 1.0\ \mathrm{M} \) KOH solution to demonstrate their versatile applications. As shown in Fig. 5(a), \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) also exhibits the best HER activity among the ternary phosphides. To afford a current density of \( 10\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2} \), a small overpotential of \( 54\ \mathrm{mV} \) is required for \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) electrode (Fig. 5(c)). Tafel slope of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) \( (59\ \mathrm{mV\cdot dec}^{-1}) \) is also close to that of \( \mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C} \) \( (52\ \mathrm{mV\cdot dec}^{-1}) \), which is much smaller than those of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) and \( \mathrm{CoWP}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \), suggesting its superior HER kinetics in alkaline solution. It is noted that \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) can also perform well against high durability under alkaline conditions. As illustrated in Fig. 5(d), the required voltage only experiences a slight increase when a current density of \( 20\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2} \) is applied. Its excellent stability can further be confirmed by LSV curve of \( \mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C} \) after 50 hours' electrocatalysis (Fig. S11 in the ESM).
In order to gain insights into the HER catalytic mechanism of Co-W phosphides, systematic DFT calculations of hydrogen adsorption free energy \(\left(\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}^{+}}\right)\)are implemented to explore the effect of Co/W ratio (supporting information) since \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}^{+}}\)is widely accepted as a good descriptor of materials for catalyzing hydrogen generation following either the Volmer–Tafel or the Heyrovsky mechanism [43]. In the typical DFT calculation, \(\mathrm{CoWP}_{2}\) (0 0 1) surface with different terminations are selected, named T1 (first layer: P; second layer Co); T2 (first layer: P; second layer W); T3 (first layer: W; second layer P); T4 (first layer: Co; second layer P). The calculated results (Fig. S13(a) in the ESM) indicate that the (0 0 1) T4 facet presents the lowest surface energy \((3.18\;\mathrm{J}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{2})\). As illustrated in Fig. S13(b) in the ESM, it is found that only Co atoms act as the active sites and the corresponding free energy for hydrogen evolution is \(-0.22~\mathrm{eV}\). As a consequence, the (0 0 1) T4 facet is chosen during the following calculation. Based on the experimental results, the W doping of 1/9 and 2/9 is considered. Figure S14 in the ESM shows one configuration for 1/9 W doping (named Co(W)) and three respective configurations for 2/9 W doping (named Co2(W2)1, Co2(W2)2 and Co2(W2)3), with the corresponding \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}^{+}}\)values for possible active sites in those
configurations being also calculated and depicted. Among the three configurations of 1/9 W doping, Co(W) Co2 is found to possess an ideal \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}^{+}}\)value of \(0.0~\mathrm{eV}\), where Co2 is a perfect active site for the \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)absorption. In contrast, the most optimum \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}^{+}}\)value in 2/9 configurations is \(-0.03~\mathrm{eV}\) of Co2(W2)2 Co2, which is also superior to the value \(-0.07~\mathrm{eV}\) of Pt (111) and \(-0.22~\mathrm{eV}\) of (111) T4. The DFT calculation results above demonstrate that W doping can be an effective approach to create active site for \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)absorption to facilitate the high-efficiency of hydrogen evolution reaction. The main reason is that the incorporation of W tends to bring a stronger electrondonating capacity of the diphosphide dumbbells of \(\mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}\), which will further lead to the downshift in energy level of eg orbitals and decrease the \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}^{+}}\)value at Co2 site. This is mainly due to the slight change of the surrounding electronic environment. As indicated in Fig. S15, the density of states for the active site shows little changes, while the electronic of neighbor metal sites becomes localized. The localized electronic will weak the interaction between hydrogen and neighbor metal sites, thus reduce the adsorption energy.
As a vital step of HER process, the water dissociation on different surfaces were also calculated and illustrated in Fig. 6(f). Comparing with \(\mathrm{CoWP}_{2}\) (001)T4, the thermodynamic barriers for water dissociation are distinctly reduced while the kinetics barriers are increased for Co(W) on Co site, indicating that Co site is not the active sites for water dissociation due to the high kinetics barrier \((1.26~\mathrm{eV})\). In contrast, it is noted that the thermodynamic and kinetics barriers for water dissociation are both simultaneously reduced for Co(W) on W site, which demonstrates that W site could facilitate the water dissociation due to the optimized surrounding electronic environment.
## 4 Conclusion
The paper reports a facile strategy to fabricate cobalt tungsten phosphides \(\left(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{W}_{2-x}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\right)\) for HER catalysis. Beneficial from the hollow structure formed by nanosheets, the MOFs derived polyhedrons could provide a high surface area and expose more active sites facilitating the transportation of electrolyte and generated gas. The as-synthesized \(\mathrm{Co}_{0.9}\mathrm{W}_{1.1}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) exhibits remarkable HER activity in both acid and alkaline media. The required overpotentials at a current density of \(10~\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) in \(0.5~\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) and 1M KOH are only 35 and \(54~\mathrm{mV}\), respectively. DFT calculations indicate that the enhanced HER performances are attributed to the surface W-doping, which could improve
the hydrogen absorption free energy \(\left(\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}^{-}}\right)\)as well as the thermodynamic and kinetics barriers for water dissociation. The strategy herein can be extended to the synthesis of other ternary and Co/W compounds for various applications in energy and green environment fields.
